{
 "meta": {
  "title": "Atmospheric Ledger \u2014 CO2 in the air, and the warming it drives",
  "latest_year": 2025,
  "first_year": 1959,
  "units": "CO2 in ppm (parts per million, mole fraction in dry air); CH4 in ppb; temperature anomaly in \u00b0C vs 1951\u20131980; carbon budget in GtC/yr (1 GtC = 3.664 GtCO2; 1 ppm CO2 = 2.124 GtC = 7.79 GtCO2)",
  "sources": {
   "co2": "NOAA Global Monitoring Laboratory, Trends in atmospheric CO2 (Mauna Loa annual means and global annual means; Lan et al.), gml.noaa.gov/webdata/ccgg/trends/co2/",
   "ch4": "NOAA Global Monitoring Laboratory, Trends in atmospheric CH4 (global annual means), gml.noaa.gov/webdata/ccgg/trends/ch4/",
   "temp": "NASA GISTEMP v4, global mean surface temperature anomaly, land-ocean (J-D annual means, baseline 1951-1980; Lenssen et al. 2019, doi:10.1029/2018JD029522), data.giss.nasa.gov/gistemp/tabledata_v4/GLB.Ts+dSST.csv",
   "budget": "Friedlingstein et al. 2025, Global Carbon Budget 2025, Earth Syst. Sci. Data 18, 3211-3288, doi:10.5194/essd-18-3211-2026; data doi:10.18160/GCP-2025 (spreadsheet via ICOS Carbon Portal). Budget quantities run through 2024 only.",
   "gcos": "GCOS Essential Climate Variables, atmospheric domain (16 ECVs), gcos.wmo.int/site/global-climate-observing-system-gcos/essential-climate-variables (accessed 2026-11-27)",
   "forcing": "IPCC AR6 WG1, Chapter 7 (Forster et al. 2021), effective radiative forcing 1750-2019, doi:10.1017/9781009157896.009",
   "preindustrial": "CO2 278 ppm and CH4 722 ppb, pre-industrial (1750) references, IPCC AR6"
  },
  "notes": [
   "Headline CO2 and temperature values are computed from the downloaded NOAA GML and NASA GISTEMP files by this script; nothing on the page is hand-typed from a table.",
   "The Global Carbon Budget 2025 series ends in 2024, so the airborne fraction and the sink figures run through 2024; the 2025 CO2 growth (2.84 ppm globally) is the observed NOAA value, converted at 2.124 GtC per ppm for the cumulative total.",
   "No values on this page are read from a chart. Every figure comes from a downloaded data file or a cited paper value.",
   "Sign convention: atmospheric growth is POSITIVE (carbon added to the air). The airborne fraction is the share of total human emissions (fossil + land-use change) that stayed in the atmosphere in that year.",
   "Temperature anomalies are relative to the 1951-1980 baseline (GISTEMP). Warming since 1880-1899 is the difference between the latest anomaly and the 1880-1899 mean anomaly (-0.23 \u00b0C)."
  ]
 },
